    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-old man with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and a stroke 2 years ago is evaluated for worsening cognitive function. The family reports a **stepwise decline** in the patient''''s memory, executive function, and motor planning over the past year, coinciding with episodes of focal weakness. Cognitive testing shows a MoCA score of **17/30**. A brain MRI reveals **multiple lacunar infarcts and diffuse white matter hyperintensities**. Which of the following is the primary focus of management for this patient''''s cognitive decline?', '["Initiation of high-dose corticosteroids to reduce cerebral vasculitis and white matter edema", "Aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification (blood pressure control, antiplatelet therapy, statin therapy) to prevent further ischemic events", "Cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy as a curative treatment to reverse white matter ischemic damage", "Empiric levodopa-carbidopa therapy to improve executive planning and motor speed", "Urgent ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement to relieve CSF pressure built up in the ventricles"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient''''s clinical presentation—a history of stroke, cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia), a **stepwise decline** in cognitive function, and **multiple lacunar infarcts and diffuse white matter hyperintensities** (subcortical white matter hyperintensities, lacunar infarcts) on MRI—points to **Vascular Dementia (VaD)**. VaD is characterized by cognitive deficits that correlate temporally with cerebrovascular events. Unlike the insidious, gradual decline of Alzheimer''''s, VaD often progresses in a stepwise or jagged manner. The primary focus of management is **secondary prevention of stroke** through aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification (blood pressure target <130/80 mmHg, antiplatelets like aspirin, high-intensity statins, and glycemic control) to stop or slow the progression of further ischemic brain injury. Cholinesterase inhibitors have shown limited benefits and are not curative.'),
