ug should be discontinued or tapered, and bradycardia resolved, before considering alternative cognitive therapies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-old man with moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''''s disease is started on **Memantine 28 mg** daily as an add-on therapy to Donepezil. The family asks how this medication works and if it is appropriate for the patient. Which of the following describes the mechanism of action of Memantine and its clinical indication?', '["GABA-A receptor agonist that enhances inhibitory neurotransmission and reduces anxiety; indicated for all stages of vascular dementia", "Beta-amyloid antibody that clears senile plaques from the cerebral vasculature; indicated for frontotemporal dementia", "Uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist that blocks pathological tonic levels of glutamate, preventing excitotoxic neuronal death; indicated for moderate-to-severe (but not mild) Alzheimer''''s disease", "Monoamine oxidase inhibitor that increases dopamine concentration in the basal ganglia; indicated for Lewy body dementia with motor symptoms", "Reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that increases cholinergic transmission in the neocortex; indicated for mild cognitive impairment"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Memantine** is a low-affinity, uncompetitive **N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor antagonist**. Pathologically in Alzheimer''''s disease, chronic overexposure to **glutamate** leads to tonic activation of NMDA receptors, allowing excessive calcium influx into neurons (excitotoxicity), which leads to synaptic dysfunction and cell death. Memantine blocks these open NMDA receptor channels under pathological conditions, while still allowing normal, rapid physiological bursts of glutamate needed for learning and memory. Memantine is clinically indicated **only for moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''''s disease** (either as monotherapy or combined with a cholinesterase inhibitor like Donepezil). It is not indicated for mild AD or Mild Cognitive Impairment, as studies have shown no significant clinical benefit in those populations.'),
