    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-old female with mild Alzheimer''''s disease was started on **Donepezil 10 mg** daily 3 weeks ago. The patient is brought to the clinic due to recurrent episodes of lightheadedness, nausea, abdominal cramping, and one episode of syncope while standing. Physical exam is notable for a heart rate of **53 bpm**. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this patient''''s symptoms, and what is the Beers Criteria warning regarding this drug class?', '["Atypical allergic hypersensitivity causing proximal muscle wasting and severe orthostatic hypotension", "Cholinergic excess causing bradycardia and gastrointestinal hypermotility; the Beers Criteria recommends caution and monitoring in patients with pre-existing conduction disease (e.g., sick sinus syndrome, bradycardia) due to risk of syncope and falls", "Anticholinergic toxicity causing urinary retention and paralytic ileus; discontinue the medication and initiate atropine therapy immediately", "Drug-induced hepatic encephalopathy causing autonomic neuropathy; manage by checking ammonia levels and initiating lactulose", "NMDA receptor over-activation causing calcium influx and peripheral vasoconstriction; manage by switching to memantine monotherapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient is experiencing adverse effects from **Donepezil**, a **cholinesterase inhibitor**. By inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, these drugs increase acetylcholine levels in the central and peripheral systems. This leads to **cholinergic excess** (parasympathetic activation), commonly causing gastrointestinal side effects (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal cramps) and vagally-mediated **bradycardia (HR of 53 bpm)**. In older adults, bradycardia can lead to orthostatic hypotension, syncope, and falls. The **Beers Criteria cautions** against the use of cholinesterase inhibitors in patients with a history of **bradycardia, syncope, or conduction block (e.g., sick sinus syndrome, second- or third-degree AV block)**. The drug should be discontinued or tapered, and bradycardia resolved, before considering alternative cognitive therapies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-old woman with moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''''s disease is started on **Memantine 28 mg** daily as an add-on therapy to Donepezil. The family asks how this medication works and if it is appropriate for the patient. Which of the following describes the mechanism of action of Memantine and its clinical indication?', '["Uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist that blocks pathological tonic levels of glutamate, preventing excitotoxic neuronal death; indicated for moderate-to-severe (but not mild) Alzheimer''''s disease", "Reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that increases cholinergic transmission in the neocortex; indicated for mild cognitive impairment", "Beta-amyloid antibody that clears senile plaques from the cerebral vasculature; indicated for frontotemporal dementia", "Monoamine oxidase inhibitor that increases dopamine concentration in the basal ganglia; indicated for Lewy body dementia with motor symptoms", "GABA-A receptor agonist that enhances inhibitory neurotransmission and reduces anxiety; indicated for all stages of vascular dementia"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Memantine** is a low-affinity, uncompetitive **N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor antagonist**. Pathologically in Alzheimer''''s disease, chronic overexposure to **glutamate** leads to tonic activation of NMDA receptors, allowing excessive calcium influx into neurons (excitotoxicity), which leads to synaptic dysfunction and cell death. Memantine blocks these open NMDA receptor channels under pathological conditions, while still allowing normal, rapid physiological bursts of glutamate needed for learning and memory. Memantine is clinically indicated **only for moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''''s disease** (either as monotherapy or combined with a cholinesterase inhibitor like Donepezil). It is not indicated for mild AD or Mild Cognitive Impairment, as studies have shown no significant clinical benefit in those populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-old man is brought to the clinic by a family member due to progressive memory loss over the past 5 years. The family reports that the patient frequently misplaces items, repeats questions, and has difficulty managing finances. Cognitive testing reveals a MoCA score of **21/30**. A brain MRI shows bilateral **medial temporal lobe and parietal** atrophy. Which of the following describes the characteristic histopathological findings and first-line pharmacological treatment for this condition?', '["Perivascular lymphocytic cuffs and demyelinating plaques; treat with interferon-beta", "Extracellular amyloid-beta plaques and intracellular hyperphosphorylated tau neurofibrillary tangles; treat with cholinesterase inhibitors (e.g., Donepezil)", "Alpha-synuclein cytoplasmic inclusions (Lewy bodies) in the substantia nigra; treat with high-dose levodopa/carbidopa", "Spongiform encephalopathy with vacuolation of neurons; treat with acyclovir and corticosteroids", "Ubiquitin and tau-positive inclusions in the frontotemporal lobes; treat with high-dose atypical antipsychotics"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient''''s clinical presentation—insidious onset, slowly progressive memory impairment, and bilateral **medial temporal lobe and parietal** (hippocampal/temporoparietal) atrophy on brain MRI—is diagnostic of **Alzheimer''''s disease (AD)**, the most common cause of dementia in older adults. The hallmark histopathological features of AD include: (1) **Extracellular amyloid-beta plaques** (derived from amyloid precursor protein cleavage), and (2) **Intracellular neurofibrillary tangles** consisting of **hyperphosphorylated tau protein**. First-line pharmacological management for mild-to-moderate AD includes **cholinesterase inhibitors** (Donepezil, Galantamine, or Rivastigmine) to increase acetylcholine levels in the synaptic cleft. Memantine, an NMDA receptor antagonist, can be added for moderate-to-severe disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-old woman with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and a stroke 2 years ago is evaluated for worsening cognitive function. The family reports a **stepwise decline** in the patient''''s memory, executive function, and motor planning over the past year, coinciding with episodes of focal weakness. Cognitive testing shows a MoCA score of **19/30**. A brain MRI reveals **multiple lacunar infarcts and diffuse white matter hyperintensities**. Which of the following is the primary focus of management for this patient''''s cognitive decline?', '["Empiric levodopa-carbidopa therapy to improve executive planning and motor speed", "Urgent ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement to relieve CSF pressure built up in the ventricles", "Aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification (blood pressure control, antiplatelet therapy, statin therapy) to prevent further ischemic events", "Initiation of high-dose corticosteroids to reduce cerebral vasculitis and white matter edema", "Cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy as a curative treatment to reverse white matter ischemic damage"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient''''s clinical presentation—a history of stroke, cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia), a **stepwise decline** in cognitive function, and **multiple lacunar infarcts and diffuse white matter hyperintensities** (subcortical white matter hyperintensities, lacunar infarcts) on MRI—points to **Vascular Dementia (VaD)**. VaD is characterized by cognitive deficits that correlate temporally with cerebrovascular events. Unlike the insidious, gradual decline of Alzheimer''''s, VaD often progresses in a stepwise or jagged manner. The primary focus of management is **secondary prevention of stroke** through aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification (blood pressure target <130/80 mmHg, antiplatelets like aspirin, high-intensity statins, and glycemic control) to stop or slow the progression of further ischemic brain injury. Cholinesterase inhibitors have shown limited benefits and are not curative.');
